      "message": "We should add more test coverage. Here\u0027s code generated by Gemini. Review for correctness and completeness in coverage:\n\n---\n\n### Rationale: Fakes vs. Mocks in Embedded Tooling\n\nTransitioning from a strict Mock (`MockUsbComm`) to a Fake (`FakeUsbComm`) combined with a Test Fixture (`TEST_F`) provides significant advantages for testing hardware protocols:\n\n* **Decoupling from Implementation Details:** Mocks verify *behavior* by asserting exactly how many times `Send` or `Receive` were called. If you later optimize the flasher to merge two `Send` calls into one, the mock test breaks. A Fake verifies *state* (the data on the wire), meaning the test remains stable as long as the correct bytes are ultimately transmitted.\n* **Reduced Setup Boilerplate:** Test Fixtures allow you to initialize the `BootromComm` object, set the cryptographic algorithm, and generate the AES key once in a `SetUp()` method, rather than repeating it in every test case.\n* **Streamlined Data Injection:** Testing operations like chunked reads or AES wrapping is tedious with a Mock because you must write custom lambdas for `.WillOnce()`. With a Fake, you simply push the expected response packet into a FIFO queue.\n\n***Note on Mocks:*** While Fakes handle data transformations beautifully, Mocks remain the superior tool for testing timeouts and infinite loops. I have retained `MockUsbComm` for the `FlashReadyTimeout` test to demonstrate how to simulate a hardware device that is \"stuck\" indefinitely.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The USB Fake (`fake_usb_comm.h`)\n\nThis Fake acts as a simulated USB endpoint pair. It maintains an input FIFO queue for device responses and logs all host transmissions for verification.\n\n```cpp\n/* Copyright 2026 The ChromiumOS Authors\n * Use of this source code is governed by a BSD-style license that can be\n * found in the LICENSE file.\n */\n#ifndef UTIL_EGIS_FAKE_USB_COMM_H_\n#define UTIL_EGIS_FAKE_USB_COMM_H_\n\n#include \u003calgorithm\u003e\n#include \u003cqueue\u003e\n#include \u003cspan\u003e\n#include \u003cvector\u003e\n\n#include \"usb_interface.h\"\n\nnamespace egis {\n\nclass FakeUsbComm : public UsbInterface {\n public:\n  // --- Test API ---\n  \n  // Push a packet into the Fake\u0027s device-to-host FIFO queue\n  void PushDeviceResponse(std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e data) {\n    device_to_host_.push(std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e(data.begin(), data.end()));\n  }\n\n  // Retrieve the log of all data the host sent to the Fake\n  const std::vector\u003cstd::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e\u003e\u0026 GetHostTransmissions() const {\n    return host_to_device_;\n  }\n\n  // Clear the internal state\n  void Reset() {\n    device_to_host_ \u003d {};\n    host_to_device_.clear();\n  }\n\n  // --- UsbInterface Implementation ---\n  \n  std::expected\u003cvoid, UsbError\u003e Connect() override { \n    return {}; \n  }\n\n  std::expected\u003cvoid, UsbError\u003e Send(\n      std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e data,\n      std::chrono::milliseconds /*timeout*/) override {\n    host_to_device_.push_back(std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e(data.begin(), data.end()));\n    return {};\n  }\n\n  std::expected\u003cint, UsbError\u003e Receive(\n      std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e data,\n      std::chrono::milliseconds /*timeout*/) override {\n    if (device_to_host_.empty()) {\n      return std::unexpected(UsbError::kTimeout);\n    }\n    \n    auto\u0026 next_packet \u003d device_to_host_.front();\n    size_t copy_size \u003d std::min(data.size(), next_packet.size());\n    std::ranges::copy(std::span(next_packet).first(copy_size), data.begin());\n    \n    // Simulate a real FIFO: remove the consumed bytes\n    next_packet.erase(next_packet.begin(), next_packet.begin() + copy_size);\n    if (next_packet.empty()) {\n      device_to_host_.pop();\n    }\n    \n    return static_cast\u003cint\u003e(copy_size);\n  }\n\n private:\n  std::queue\u003cstd::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e\u003e device_to_host_;\n  std::vector\u003cstd::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e\u003e host_to_device_;\n};\n\n}  // namespace egis\n\n#endif  // UTIL_EGIS_FAKE_USB_COMM_H_\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Refactored Tests (`bootrom_test.cc`)\n\nThis updated test file leverages the `BootromCommTest` fixture to eliminate boilerplate, uses the Fake for all standard protocol verifications, and reserves the Mock for the specific polling timeout scenario.\n\n```cpp\n/* Copyright 2026 The ChromiumOS Authors\n * Use of this source code is governed by a BSD-style license that can be\n * found in the LICENSE file.\n */\n#include \"bootrom.h\"\n\n#include \u003cgmock/gmock.h\u003e\n#include \u003cgtest/gtest.h\u003e\n\n#include \u003calgorithm\u003e\n#include \u003carray\u003e\n#include \u003cvector\u003e\n\n#include \"fake_usb_comm.h\"\n#include \"mock_usb_comm.h\"\n\nnamespace egis {\nnamespace {\n\nusing ::testing::_;\nusing ::testing::Return;\n\nconstexpr uint32_t kTestVersion \u003d 0x01020304;\nconstexpr uint32_t kTestMaxCmdSize \u003d 1024;\nconstexpr uint32_t kTestMaxFwUpgradeSize \u003d 2048;\nconstexpr uint8_t kTestKeyFillValue \u003d 0x77;\n\nconstexpr auto kTestPlaintext \u003d std::to_array\u003cuint8_t\u003e({0x11, 0x22, 0x33, 0x44});\n\n// \u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\n// Test Fixture: Uses FakeUsbComm for state-based data verification\n// \u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\nclass BootromCommTest : public ::testing::Test {\n protected:\n  void SetUp() override {\n    // Standard setup: AES-GCM with a known key\n    test_key_.fill(kTestKeyFillValue);\n    brom_.SetWrappedAlgo(EgisCryptoType::kAesGcm);\n    brom_.SetAesKey(test_key_);\n  }\n\n  // Generates a valid AES response using the fixture\u0027s internal key\n  std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e MakeMockAesResponse(std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e plaintext, \n                                           bool corrupt_tag \u003d false) {\n    std::array\u003cuint8_t, crypto::kAesGcmIvSize\u003e iv{};\n    std::array\u003cuint8_t, crypto::kAesGcmTagSize\u003e tag{};\n    std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e ciphertext(plaintext.size());\n    uint32_t aad \u003d ToLittleEndian(EgisBootrom::kChipIdAad);\n\n    EXPECT_TRUE(crypto::GenerateGcmIv(iv).has_value());\n    EXPECT_TRUE(crypto::AesGcmEncrypt(test_key_, iv, AsBytes(aad), \n                                      plaintext, ciphertext, tag).has_value());\n\n    if (corrupt_tag) tag[0] ^\u003d 0xFF;\n\n    BootromComm::SecureResponseHeader hdr{\n        .status \u003d 0,\n        .payload_len \u003d ToLittleEndian(static_cast\u003cuint32_t\u003e(ciphertext.size()))};\n\n    auto resp_packet \u003d PackMessage(hdr, iv);\n    resp_packet.insert(resp_packet.end(), tag.begin(), tag.end());\n    resp_packet.insert(resp_packet.end(), ciphertext.begin(), ciphertext.end());\n    return resp_packet;\n  }\n\n  // Generates a valid SHA response\n  std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e MakeMockShaResponse(std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e payload) {\n    BootromComm::SecureResponseHeader hdr{\n        .status \u003d 0,\n        .payload_len \u003d ToLittleEndian(static_cast\u003cuint32_t\u003e(payload.size()))};\n    return PackMessage(hdr, payload);\n  }\n\n  FakeUsbComm fake_usb_;\n  BootromComm brom_{fake_usb_};\n  std::array\u003cuint8_t, crypto::kAes256KeySize\u003e test_key_{};\n};\n\n// --- Fake-Based Tests ---\n\nTEST_F(BootromCommTest, GetBootromInfoSuccess) {\n  // Overriding default algorithm for this specific test\n  brom_.SetWrappedAlgo(EgisCryptoType::kSha256);\n\n  EgisBootrom::BootromInfoResponse info{\n      .version \u003d ToLittleEndian(kTestVersion),\n      .max_cmd_size \u003d ToLittleEndian(kTestMaxCmdSize),\n      .max_fw_upgrade_size \u003d ToLittleEndian(kTestMaxFwUpgradeSize)};\n  \n  uint16_t status \u003d 0;\n  fake_usb_.PushDeviceResponse(PackMessage(status, AsBytes(info)));\n\n  auto res \u003d brom_.GetBootromInfo();\n  ASSERT_TRUE(res.has_value());\n  EXPECT_EQ(res-\u003eversion, kTestVersion);\n}\n\nTEST_F(BootromCommTest, SecureCommandAesSuccess) {\n  EgisBootrom::MemReadCommand read_cmd{\n      .address \u003d ToLittleEndian(EgisBootrom::kFlashBaseAddr),\n      .access_size \u003d EgisBootrom::kAccessSizeUint8,\n      .count \u003d ToLittleEndian(static_cast\u003cuint32_t\u003e(kTestPlaintext.size()))};\n\n  fake_usb_.PushDeviceResponse(MakeMockAesResponse(kTestPlaintext));\n\n  auto res \u003d brom_.SendStruct(read_cmd);\n\n  ASSERT_TRUE(res.has_value());\n  EXPECT_TRUE(std::ranges::equal(kTestPlaintext, res.value()));\n  // We can verify that the host actually sent exactly one command packet\n  EXPECT_EQ(fake_usb_.GetHostTransmissions().size(), 1);\n}\n\nTEST_F(BootromCommTest, SecureCommandAesAuthenticationFailure) {\n  EgisBootrom::MemReadCommand read_cmd{\n      .address \u003d ToLittleEndian(EgisBootrom::kFlashBaseAddr),\n      .access_size \u003d EgisBootrom::kAccessSizeUint8,\n      .count \u003d ToLittleEndian(static_cast\u003cuint32_t\u003e(kTestPlaintext.size()))};\n\n  // Push a corrupted response\n  fake_usb_.PushDeviceResponse(MakeMockAesResponse(kTestPlaintext, /*corrupt_tag\u003d*/true));\n\n  auto res \u003d brom_.SendStruct(read_cmd);\n\n  ASSERT_FALSE(res.has_value());\n  EXPECT_EQ(res.error(), BootromError::kGcmAuthenticationFailed);\n}\n\nTEST_F(BootromCommTest, SendBootromCommandEmptyBuffer) {\n  auto res \u003d brom_.SendStruct(std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e{});\n  ASSERT_FALSE(res.has_value());\n  EXPECT_EQ(res.error(), BootromError::kBufferTooSmall);\n}\n\nTEST_F(BootromCommTest, WriteFlashExceedsLimit) {\n  std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e massive_payload(BootromComm::kMaxFlashWriteSize + 1, 0xFF);\n  auto res \u003d brom_.WriteFlash(0x0000, massive_payload);\n  \n  ASSERT_FALSE(res.has_value());\n  EXPECT_EQ(res.error(), BootromError::kPayloadSizeExceedsLimit);\n}\n\nTEST_F(BootromCommTest, ReadFlashChunking) {\n  brom_.SetWrappedAlgo(EgisCryptoType::kSha256);\n  const uint32_t total_size \u003d BootromComm::kMaxFlashReadSize + 1024; // 5KB total\n\n  // Push the two distinct chunks the device will respond with\n  std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e chunk1(BootromComm::kMaxFlashReadSize, 0xAA);\n  std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e chunk2(1024, 0xBB);\n  \n  fake_usb_.PushDeviceResponse(MakeMockShaResponse(chunk1));\n  fake_usb_.PushDeviceResponse(MakeMockShaResponse(chunk2));\n\n  auto res \u003d brom_.ReadFlash(0x0000, total_size);\n  \n  ASSERT_TRUE(res.has_value());\n  EXPECT_EQ(res.value().size(), total_size);\n  EXPECT_EQ(res.value()[0], 0xAA);\n  EXPECT_EQ(res.value()[BootromComm::kMaxFlashReadSize], 0xBB);\n  \n  // Verify the host properly chunked the requests into two sends\n  EXPECT_EQ(fake_usb_.GetHostTransmissions().size(), 2);\n}\n\n// \u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\n// Mock-Based Test: Used specifically for simulating hardware timeouts\n// \u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\n\nTEST(BootromCommTimeoutTest, FlashReadyTimeout) {\n  MockUsbComm mock_usb;\n  BootromComm brom(mock_usb);\n  brom.SetWrappedAlgo(EgisCryptoType::kSha256);\n\n  // Allow all WriteReg/ReadReg transmissions to pass without error\n  EXPECT_CALL(mock_usb, Send(_, _))\n      .WillRepeatedly(Return(std::expected\u003cvoid, UsbError\u003e{}));\n\n  // Force ReadReg (which wraps GetSr1) to constantly return a WIP bit of 1.\n  // Using a mock here is critical to simulate an infinite loop of bad responses.\n  EXPECT_CALL(mock_usb, Receive(_, _))\n      .WillRepeatedly([](std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e out_buffer, auto) {\n        uint32_t stuck_sr1 \u003d EgisBootrom::kFlashSr1Wip; // Bit 0 is high\n        \n        BootromComm::SecureResponseHeader hdr{\n            .status \u003d 0,\n            .payload_len \u003d ToLittleEndian(static_cast\u003cuint32_t\u003e(sizeof(stuck_sr1)))};\n        \n        auto resp \u003d PackMessage(hdr, AsBytes(ToLittleEndian(stuck_sr1)));\n        std::ranges::copy(resp, out_buffer.begin());\n        return static_cast\u003cint\u003e(resp.size());\n      });\n\n  // DisableWp requires waiting for the flash to be ready.\n  // Because our mock constantly returns WIP\u003d1, this should eventually timeout.\n  auto res \u003d brom.DisableWp();\n  \n  ASSERT_FALSE(res.has_value());\n  EXPECT_EQ(res.error(), BootromError::kFlashReadyTimeout);\n}\n\n}  // namespace\n}  // namespace egis\n\n```",
