      "message": "**File:** `util/egis/ec_comm.cc`\n\nYou are using `reinterpret_cast` to cast an underlying byte array to a struct pointer to modify a field:\n\n```c\n  auto *hdr \u003d reinterpret_cast\u003cEcHostCommandRequest *\u003e(cmd_bytes_.data());\n  hdr-\u003echecksum \u003d static_cast\u003cuint8_t\u003e(-sum);\n\n```\n\nWhile `__attribute__((packed))` often makes compilers forgive this, casting a byte array to an object type and mutating it violates C++ strict aliasing rules, resulting in Undefined Behavior.\n\n**Remediation:** \n\nGoogle C++ Style heavily discourages `reinterpret_cast` except in very specific hardware/serialization boundaries, and mutating through an aliased pointer is Undefined Behavior in standard C++. Since you are operating on a `std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e` that you just packed, utilize `\u003ccstddef\u003e` and `offsetof` to mutate the exact byte safely.\n\n```c\n#include \u003ccstddef\u003e // Ensure this is included\n\n  void UpdateChecksum() {\n    uint32_t sum \u003d 0;\n    for (uint8_t byte : cmd_bytes_) sum +\u003d byte;\n\n    // Safely update the checksum byte directly in the vector\n    // EcHostCommandRequest must be a Standard Layout Type for offsetof\n    cmd_bytes_[offsetof(EcHostCommandRequest, checksum)] \u003d static_cast\u003cuint8_t\u003e(-sum);\n  }\n\n```",
